## H I G H L I G H T S
- Conductive \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) was synthesized as a sensing electrode of enzyme-free ascorbic acid detection.  - A portable ascorbic acid detection system was developed.  - A wide linear range was obtained with high sensitivity and a low detection limit.  - This stable portable sensor could be applied to detecting ascorbic acid levels in human sweat.
G R A P H I C A L A B S T R A C T
A sensitive, portable, enzyme-free ascorbic acid detection system based on the use of conductive, highly crystalline \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) as a sensing material was designed. This system was integrated with a smartphone via an application-specific integrated circuit including Bluetooth 5.0 capabilities, showing good electrocatalytic performance to accurately monitor the ascorbic acid level in sweat.
## A R T I C L E I N F O
Article history:  Received 26 December 2021  Revised 8 February 2022  Accepted 11 February 2022  Available online 16 February 2022
Keyword:  Conductive metal–organic framework  High crystallinity  Enzymeless ascorbic acid sensor  Electrochemical portable detection system
## A B S T R A C T
Conductive metal–organic frameworks can provide unique porous structures, large pore volumes, many catalytically active sites and high crystallinity, and so are becoming increasingly important and attractive as electrocatalytic materials. The present work synthesized nanorods of the conductive compound \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(2,3,6,7,10,11)\)-hexaiminotriphenylene\(_{2}\) \((\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2})\) with a high degree of crystallinity from HITP ligands and \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) ions. Screen-printed electrodes made with this material were employed to fabricate an enzyme-free sensor for the detection of ascorbic acid (AA). The sensor exhibited good catalytic activity during the electrocatalytic analysis of AA in alkaline media, attributed to the synergistic effect of highly active \(\mathrm{Ni}-\mathrm{N}_{4}\) catalytic sites in the nanorods, the two-dimensional superimposed honeycomb lattice of the \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\), and the large specific surface area of this material. The latter property facilitated efficient electron transfer during catalytic oxidation. A portable electrochemical AA detection system was developed using \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) as the electrode material together with application-specific integrated circuits and a smartphone application with App. Good sensing performance was obtained, including a wide linear range \((2-200\ \upmu\mathrm{M})\) with high sensitivity \((0.814\ \upmu\mathrm{A}\ \upmu\mathrm{M}^{-1}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2})\), and low detection limit \((1\ \upmu\mathrm{M})\). This
system can be used to monitor AA levels and trends in sweat to assess vitamin C intake as a part of personal health management.
