## 1. Introduction
Ascorbic acid (AA), also known as vitamin C, is an anti-scorbutic agent and an essential nutrient for the human body. The vitamin C concentration in human sweat ranges from 10 to \(50\,\upmu\mathrm{mol}/\mathrm{L}[1]\). The normal concentration range of vitamin C in the human body in daily life is 34 to \(114\,\upmu\mathrm{mol}/\mathrm{L}[2]\). As an antioxidant, AA can reduce the incidence of cancer by effectively scavenging free radicals in the body[3]. Moreover, AA maintains enzyme activity in the body and can prevent the production of toxic substances by inhibiting peroxidation. AA plays a number of important roles in biological metabolism, such as the synthesis of adrenaline and neurotransmitters and the digestion of amino acids[4,5]. AA can also improve the immunity of the body. For these reasons, the quantitative analysis of AA using portable or wearable systems is of interest. Various methods have been devised for the detection of AA, including electrochemical, chromatographic, fluorescent, and chemiluminescent techniques[6]. Among these, electrochemical-based methods are very attractive because they provide high sensitivity and fast reaction times based on either bio-enzymatic or non-enzymatic analysis. In enzymatic biosensors, AA is oxidized to dehydroascorbic acid by ascorbate oxidase under oxygen but it is challenging to immobilize the enzyme in such systems[7]. These units are also very sensitive to environmental conditions such as pH and temperature [8,9]. Considering the limited stability and reproducibility of bioenzymatic sensors, there have been attempts to prepare electrochemical AA sensors without enzymes[3,4,10–12]. The electrocatalytic oxidation of AA, which does not require the participation of biological enzymes, provides good stability, and allows for easy storage and relatively simple sensor design[13]. However, the majority of the sensing materials at the core of such systems have low specific surface areas, minimal active catalytic sites and require complicated preparation processes. Therefore, the design of new catalytic materials having well-defined chemical compositions and porous structures is an important aspect of improving the performance of electrochemical sensors[14–16].
Over the past several decades, advances in the synthesis of nanomaterials have facilitated research outputs in many fields. In particular, carbon nanomaterials based on graphene[17], conducting polymers such as polyaniline (PANI)[18] and nano metal oxides (including \(\mathsf{C u O}[19]\), \(\mathsf{M n O}_{2}[20]\), and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}[21]\),) have improved the performance of biosensors. However, these materials generally have disadvantages, including poor sensitivity and stability, and complicated preparation processes[3,22]. Therefore, the development of nano-structured electrocatalysts with abundant active sites and large specific surfaces would be beneficial with regard to achieving high-sensitivity electrochemical sensing[23,24].
Newly developed porous materials consisting of organic ligands and metal ions or clusters self-assembled by coordination bonds, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs)[25,26], have received increasing attention in this area. The arrangement in the MOF structure has a distinct orientation and can produce different types of framework pore structures that exhibit varying optical, adsorption and electrochemical properties[27,28]. MOFs also provide the advantages of regular pore channels, low density, topological diversity, adjustable pore sizes, large specific surface areas and tailoring. These properties can be applied in many fields, including gas storage[29], chemical separation[30], light trapping[31], and chemical sensing[32]. Moreover, MOFs with well-defined atomic structures
and dispersed metal sites can provide ideal platforms for the exploration of the properties of active sites[31]. These materials can also exhibit electrocatalytic activity and so could possibly function as electroactive components of non-enzymatic electrochemical sensing platforms. Unfortunately, most MOFs are only minimally conductive, meaning that they provide high resistance to charge transfer and so demonstrate poor electrochemical properties [34,35]. For this reason, MOFs may be combined with more conductive materials or pyrolyzed to increase their poor conductivity [36]. Even so, both the doping and pyrolysis of pristine MOFs can reduce their surface areas and induce structural damage as well as modify the redox-active components of the material[37]. Therefore, the development of non-destructive approaches to enhancing the electrical conductivity of MOFs is vital.
Recently, two-dimensional (2D) frameworks have shown high charge carrier mobility and superior conductivity as a consequence of in-plane charge delocalization and lamellar extended \(\pi\)-conjugation[38,39]. In addition, 2D MOFs \((\mathsf{M}_{3}(\mathsf{H I T P})_{2}\) \((\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}\), Co or Ni, \(\mathsf{H I T P}~=~2,3,6,7,10,11\)-hexaiminotriphenylene) exhibit a honeycomb-like stacking lattice and contain benzene/triphenylene-derived linkers ortho-disubstituted with donor atoms (such as N, O and S) that provide a coordinated environment for various transition metal nodes[39,40]. These MOFs can be modified in various ways. As an example, Dinda et al. reported that the substitution of the weak base \(\mathsf{C H}_{2}\mathsf{C O O N a}\) for ammonia during the synthesis of \(\mathsf{N i}_{3}(\mathsf{H I T P})_{2}\) improved the crystallinity of this MOF. The crystallinity and high intrinsic electrical conductivity \((55.4\;\mathsf{S}/\mathsf{c m})\) of Ni-based MOFs containing HITP ligands can be further modified by using \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}/\) ligand solvent mixtures to produce MOFs[41]. As an example, Huang et al. synthesized \(\mathsf{C o}_{3}(\mathsf{H I T P})_{2}\) having an increased electrical conductivity of \(1150\;\mathrm{S/m}\) and outstanding oxygen evolution reaction activity[42]. Peng et al. produced \(\mathsf{C o}_{3}(\mathsf{H I T P})_{2}\) as a cathode catalyst for zinc-air rechargeable batteries and found that this material exhibited good energy efficiency and stability[43]. Peng et al. reported the first-ever composite film with 2D MOF / rGO heterostructure that provided a high CO evolution rate and good selectivity[44]. Recently, highly conductive MOFs have been explored as electrocatalysts for the oxygen reduction reaction in alkaline media and have shown good performance[45–49]. In contrast, the application of these materials to biomolecule sensors has rarely been reported.
The chemical analysis of sweat is an innovative approach to the monitoring of human health and provides the advantages of being non-invasive compared with traditional blood analysis[50,51]. As such, this technique is easier to use and could be employed for preventive health monitoring. Even so, the analysis of chemicals in sweat has typically involved the use of complex instruments such as chromatographs and spectrophotometers[52,53]. In recent years, the advent of portable or wearable biosensors and bioelectronics has greatly accelerated the pace of sweat research. With the advantages of miniaturization, rapid response, and suitability for archiving, portable or wearable sensors could allow noninvasive, real-time, and continuous sweat analysis[54–56]. Even so, there have been no reports regarding the application of conductive MOFs to the analysis of AA in human sweat. Therefore, it would be beneficial to develop an efficient, well-stabilized electrochemical analytical device to monitor AA in human sweat.
In the present work, we synthesized highly crystalline, conductive \(\mathsf{N i}_{3}(\mathsf{H I T P})_{2}\) for the electrochemical detection of AA, using a
solvent-thermal method. The resulting material provided the synergistic advantages of a large surface area and good electrical conductivity \((39.84\ \mathrm{S/cm})\) expected from an MOF as well as several unique properties related to isolated \(\mathrm{Ni}-\mathrm{N}_{4}\) units. These units in the MOF topology (located in stacked honeycomb lattices) promoted electron transfer during the catalytic reaction used to sense AA. Electrochemical tests performed under optimized conditions demonstrated that a \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\)/screen-printed carbon electrode (SPCE) device could be used to detect AA in alkaline media with good sensitivity, a wide linear range, and low limit of detection. The performance of this unit was determined to be superior to those of previously reported electrochemical non-enzymatic AA sensors based on MOFs and MOF derivatives. A portable system for AA detection was subsequently developed by integrating the \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}/\mathrm{SPCE}\) (as the sensing element), Bluetooth 5.0 hardware, a smartphone and application-specific integrated circuit (ASIC) to facilitate electrochemical data acquisition and analysis. This portable AA sensing system showed good performance under alkaline conditions and was able to quantity AA in sweat. Therefore, this sensor could be used to monitor AA as a predictor of health in humans.
## 2. Results and discussion
The procedure for the fabrication of conductive, highly crystalline \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) by a solvent-thermal method is shown in Fig. 1a, while the details of the synthesis are described in the Supplemental Information (SI). Fig. 1b provides diagrams and photographs of the portable electrochemical sensor and its components intended for the real-time analysis of AA. More details regarding this portable device are provided in SI. Per a previously reported procedure, conductive \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) was synthesized via a coordination reaction between \((\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COO})_{2}\mathrm{Ni}\) and HITP. During this reaction, \(\mathrm{d}-\pi\) bonds were formed between the \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) groups of the HITP ligands and \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) ions to construct a D4h coordination symmetry[57,58], as shown in Fig. 2a. The resulting MOF typically had a (2,3)-linked honeycomb-like 2D porous lattice structure. Theoretically, each conjugated ring in \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) was coordinated to six HITP ligands and six \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) ions to form pores with diameters of approximately \(1.5\ \mathrm{nm}\)[59]. These pores would be expected to promote the adsorption of AA onto the \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\), leading to the electrocatalytic oxidation of the analyte at active catalytic sites.
