Fig. 2b demonstrates that the \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) X-ray diffraction (XRD) pattern contained peaks at \(4.7^{\circ}\), \(9.5^{\circ}\), \(12.6^{\circ}\), \(16.5^{\circ}\), \(27.1^{\circ}\) and \(33.3^{\circ}\) that were consistent with the structure that has been reported in the literature[60,61]. The FTIR spectra in Fig. 2c show that the conjugated \(\pi\)-electrons of the ligands were redistributed during the formation of the \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) to form a new more highly conjugated system[43], resulting in shifts or changes in absorptions related to moieties such as \(\mathsf{C}-\mathsf{N}\) groups and benzene rings compared with those of the ligand. As examples, the peaks associated with \(-\mathrm{NH}\) and \(\mathsf{C}-\mathsf{N}\) groups were positively shifted to \(3485.4\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) and \(1306.9\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\)[62,63], respectively. As shown in Fig. 2d, a typical type I reversible adsorption isotherm plot with a Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) specific surface area of \(548.2\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\) was obtained for \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\). The average pore diameter was calculated using the Barrett-Joyner-Halenda (BJH) model to be approximately \(1.3\ \mathrm{nm}\) (Fig. 2e), which is consistent with the crystal structure of this material[58]. These data confirm that the \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) contained numerous internal pore channels that would be expected to increase the contact area between the catalyst and an electrolyte and provide efficient ion transport during the electrocatalytic oxidation of AA[64].
X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was used to further study the electronic structure and chemical composition of the sample. The Ni 2p binding energy values were determined to be \(872.6\ \mathrm{eV}\) (Ni \(2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}\)) and \(855.1\ \mathrm{eV}\) (Ni \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\)) while the corresponding satellite peaks were located at \(878.5\) and \(860.1\ \mathrm{eV}\)[65]. These data indicate that Ni was primarily in the form of \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) in the \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) structure (Fig. 2f). A N 1s signal at \(399.0\ \mathrm{eV}\) also suggested a \(\mathrm{Ni}-\mathrm{N}_{4}\) four-coordination structure that would be expected to promote electrocatalytic activity[66]. The XPS analysis also showed a lack of impurities such as NiO \((853.7\ \mathrm{eV})\), \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) \((855.6\ \mathrm{eV})\) and metallic Ni \((852.7\ \mathrm{eV})\)[67,68]. The N 1s XPS spectrum could be deconvoluted into two peaks at \(399.6\ \mathrm{eV}\) (attributed to aniline \((- \mathrm{NH}-)\)) and \(397.9\ \mathrm{eV}\) (quinonimine \((- \mathrm{N}-)\)), which are associated with the partial oxidation of \(\mathrm{HITP}[69]\) (Fig. 2g). Fig. 2h illustrates the deconvolution of the C 1s XPS spectrum to give three subpeaks: \(\mathrm{O}-\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{O}\) \((288.9\ \mathrm{eV})\), \(\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{N}\) \((287.7\ \mathrm{eV})\), \(\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{O}/\mathrm{Ni}\) \((285.3\ \mathrm{eV})\), and \(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{C}-\) \((284.6\ \mathrm{eV})\)[70]. These peaks correspond to the different states of carbon in the \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\). The XRD pattern and XPS spectrum obtained in the present work are consistent with those previously reported by Dinca et al[41]. Fig. S3f provides electrical conductivity data obtained from \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) films using the four-probe test method at room temperature. The conductivity was determined to be \(39.84\ \mathrm{S/cm}\), which is higher than the values for some previously reported MOFs[42,52,71]. The morphology of the \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) was investigated in detail by field emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM). The sample was found to consist of uniform rod-like structures with lengths of \(100-200\ \mathrm{nm}\) and widths of \(10-20\ \mathrm{nm}\) (Fig. 2i). As depicted in Fig. 2j and k, the \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) structure determined from Pawley refinement had a well-defined \(1.89\ \mathrm{nm}\) hexagonal lattice consistent with the periodic arrangement of hexamethylene triphenylene esters interconnected by square planes comprising metal centers[58] (Fig. 2l).
The electrochemical analysis of the \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}/\mathrm{SPCE}\) electrode is described in the SI. Cyclic voltammetry (CV) was used to assess the electrochemical properties of the SPCE and \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}/\mathrm{SPCE}\) with and without a \(0.5\ \mathrm{mM}\) AA concentration in the test solution at \(100\ \mathrm{mV}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}\). The results in Fig. 3a indicate that the SPCE was relatively inactive during the electrocatalytic process. In contrast, the \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}/\mathrm{SPCE}\) electrode generated a higher oxidation peak in the presence of \(0.5\ \mathrm{mM}\) AA that was attributed to the electrocatalytic oxidation of the AA. According to previous studies[72], in alkaline solutions, cathodic (0.35 V) and anodic (0.46 V) peaks arise
from the Ni (II)/Ni (III) interconversion process in \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) via the reaction shown in equation (1):
\[
\mathrm{Ni(II)_3(HITP)_2+OH^-}\rightarrow\mathrm{Ni(II)_3(HITP)_2+e^-}
\]
Fig. S6a shows that the \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}/\mathrm{SPCE}\) generated a current of \(902.2~\upmu\mathrm{A}\) that was greater than the value obtained using the SPCE \((44.6~\upmu\mathrm{A})\) , indicating a significant difference between the two electrodes. A Ni/SPCE electrode was also fabricated and applied to the electrocatalytic oxidation of AA but no significant change in the current was detected with the addition of AA (Fig. S6b). These data confirmed the necessity of including \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) ions in the conductive NiMOF, which evidently participate in the AA oxidation reaction. It is also clear that the \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}/\mathrm{SPCE}\) exhibited significant activity during the electrocatalytic oxidation of AA and this exceptional performance is attributed to three factors. First, the \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) was highly conductive and retained a large electron transfer rate constant after deposition on the SPCE. Second, this Ni-MOF contained active sites that facilitated the AA oxidation process. Lastly, the unique nanostructure of this material allowed the AA to bind to
the catalytically active sites. Fig. 3b presents the CV data obtained from the \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}/\mathrm{SPCE}\) using various AA concentrations and demonstrates that the current was increased along with the amount of AA. Note that a magnified view of the oxidation current peak is provided in Fig. S6c. As shown in Fig. 3c, a plot of AA concentration versus CV oxidation peak current \((i_{pa})\) demonstrates a linear correlation that can be described as \(i_{pa}\) \((\mathrm{mA})\;=\;0.0023\mathrm{C}\) \((\upmu\mathrm{M})\,+\,1.489\) \((\mathbf{R}^{2}\,=\,0.995)\) . It should be noted that increasing AA concentrations shifted the oxidation peak to higher potentials as a result of diffusion limiting effect related to the migration of AA on the \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}/\mathrm{SPCE}\) surface[73].
The kinetics of the electrocatalytic oxidation process were determined by varying the scan rate during the CV trials. As shown in Fig. 3d and S6d, the current peaks at both the anode \((I_{pa})\) and cathode \((I_{pc})\) increased with increases in the scan rate, while the peak oxidation potential gradually shifted in the positive direction. These results suggest a kinetic limitation to the AA oxidation reaction. Fig. 3e and S6e demonstrate that the peak currents exhibited linear correlations with \(\nu^{1/2}\) that can be described as \(i_{pa}\)
\((\mathrm{mA}) = 0.099\ \nu^{1/2} - 0.089\) \((\mathrm{R}^{2} = 0.998)\) and \(i_{pa}\) \((\mathrm{mA}) = -0.040\ \nu^{1/2} + 0.181\) \((\mathrm{R}^{2} = 0.990)\). It is apparent that the oxidation of AA on the \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}/\mathrm{SPCE}\) proceeded in conjunction with diffusion - controlled electrode kinetics [74]. This type of reaction is ideal for quantitative analysis because of the rapid electron transfer rate at the electrode, such that the reaction rate is limited by the transfer of AA to the electrode surface. As shown in Fig. 3f, the redox peak potentials \((E_{pa}\) and \(E_{pc})\) are linear to the Napierian logarithm of scan rate \((\ln\nu)\). The linear equations are \(E_{pa} = 0.0211\ \ln\nu + 0.5717\) \((\mathrm{R}^{2} = 0.993)\) and \(E_{pc} = -0.0067\ \ln\nu + 0.3745\) \((\mathrm{R}^{2} = 0.996)\), respectively. According to Lavrion equation[75](The specific calculation process is described in the SI), it is evident that the oxidation of AA was irreversible and limited by kinetics. The results also establish that the oxidation of AA involved a two-electron transfer process and that diffusion controlled the limiting current, in agreement with previous literature[76].
In alkaline solutions, \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) can be converted to \(\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}\) via the reaction in equation (1). The data in Fig. S11b demonstrate that the Ni \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) binding energy increased after the reaction, indicating an increase in the proportion of \(\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}\) in the material[77,78]. The \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(-\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) surface produced \(\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}\) ions that attracted and aggregated with the negatively charged AA. At the same time, AA was electrochemically catalyzed to generate ascorbate ions with the release of hydrogen ions followed by the formation of dehydroascorbic acid. The \(\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}\) from the MOF catalysed the oxidation of AA to produce dehydroascorbic acid \(\left(\mathsf{C}_{6}\mathsf{H}_{6}\mathsf{O}_{6}\right)\) as in equation (2)[12]. The \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{II})\) and \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{III})\) in this Ni-MOF were thus interconverted via the reactions below.
\[
\begin{array}{rl}\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{III})_3(\mathrm{HITP})_2+\mathrm{AA}+\mathrm{OH}^-\rightarrow\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{II})_3(\mathrm{HITP})_2&\\+\mathrm{dehydroascorbic~acid}&\\+\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}+\mathrm{e}&\end{array}
\]
Generally, the intrinsic reactivity of surface-active sites on a catalyst will be closely correlated with the adsorption and desorption potentials of the reaction intermediates, which in turn are determined by the energy band structures of these active sites. Thus, it is important to understand the electronic structures of the active sites when assessing the catalytic mechanism. The Ni (II)/Ni (III) transition was examined by performing electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) experiments, and the results are shown in Fig. 4a. The weak signal at \(\mathbf{g}=2.15\) was assigned to the \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) cation in the \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) structure before the reaction[79], while the strong signal at \(\mathbf{g}=2.17\) was attributed to the \(\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}\) cation in the \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) structure after the catalytic AA oxidation reaction[80]. Importantly, the signal intensity at \(\mathbf{g}=2.17\) increased after the catalytic oxidation reaction, suggesting an increase in the \(\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}\) content. This increase can likely be ascribed to the oxidation of \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) in the \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) to \(\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}\). Thus, the EPR results confirm that the chemical reaction involved a dynamic \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{II})/\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{III})\) cycle. To further confirm the catalytic mechanism, density functional theory (DFT) calculations were used to study the interfacial charge transfer at the active sites of the catalyst. The optimized vertical and parallel adsorption energies of AA adsorbed on the Ni atom in \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) were obtained by the DFT method and are presented in Fig. S7. These results show that the interaction distances between the Ni atoms and AA molecules were 2.41 and \(2.66\,\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\) for parallel and vertical adsorption, respectively, suggesting that both interactions were weak. Prior research has demonstrated that \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) in solution is often terminated by hydroxide anions \(\left(\mathrm{OH}^{-}\right)\)with the simultaneous oxidation of \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) to \(\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}[81]\). In the present work, the relationship between the catalytic activity of \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) and the Ni oxidation peaks (Fig. 3a) confirmed that the catalytic activity was derived from \(\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}\) rather than \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\). The CV data obtained from \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) (Fig. 3a) also showed the oxidation of the central \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) to \(\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}\) in conjunction with a forward bias of approximately \(0.5\ \mathrm{V}\)
-ion of AA molecules on the \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) electrode surface based on theoretical calculations is also shown. In this mechanism, the reduction of AA to dehydroascorbic acid is catalyzed by \(\mathrm{Ni}-\mathrm{N}_{4}\) active sites. Possible reaction equations for the catalytic oxidation of AA on the surface of the \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\) \((\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) electrode are shown in
Fig. 4d. The 2D structure of the conductive \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) contains highly dispersed electrons and so promotes electron transfer based on local electron-rich nodes generated by the \(\mathrm{Ni}-\mathrm{N}_{4}\) units.
The sensitivity and quantitative range of the \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}/\mathrm{SPCE}\) sensor were investigated with regard to the analysis of AA. In Fig. 5a, the response current is seen to increase with increases in the applied voltage, reaching a maximum at \(0.5\;\mathrm{V}\). The current values corresponding to different potentials are also shown in Fig. S6f. Fig. 5b represents a current–time plot obtained from the \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}/\mathrm{SPCE}\) at the optimal potential while varying the AA concentration. The inset to this figure provides a magnified section of this plot for AA concentrations of 0.5 to \(37.5\;\upmu\mathrm{M}\). The reaction time for the detection of AA using this electrode was evaluated by monitoring the steady-state current changes at different AA concentrations. The data in Fig. 5c indicate that the current stabilized rapidly at low AA concentrations (within less than 2 s), confirming that the \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) was sensitive and could detect low levels of AA. Fig. 5d plots the current density response versus AA concentration as a kind of calibration curve for the electrode.
The corresponding linear equation in the range of \(0.5–100\,\upmu\mathrm{M}\) is \(\mathrm{I}\ (\upmu\mathrm{A})\,=\,13.50\ C_{\mathrm{AA}}\ (\mathrm{mM})\,+\,0.05\) \((\mathrm{R}^{2}\,=\,0.992)\) with a sensitivity of \(191.08\;\upmu\mathrm{A}\;\mathrm{mM}^{-1}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\). The equation in the \(100{-}800~\upmu\mathrm{M}\) range was determined to be \(\mathrm{I}(\upmu\mathrm{A})=6.83\ C_{\mathrm{AA}}\ (\mathrm{mM})\,+\,0.93\) \((\mathrm{R}^{2}\,=\,0.998)\) with a sensitivity of \(96.67~\upmu\mathrm{A}~\mathrm{mM}^{-1}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\). The limit of detection (LOD) was calculated to be \(0.1~\upmu\mathrm{M}\) \((\mathrm{S}/\mathrm{N}=3)\). As demonstrated by the data in Table S1, the conductive \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) electrode exhibited good performance, including high sensitivity, a short response time and a low detection limit, compared with many electrocatalytic AA sensors based on MOFs or MOF derivatives.
To facilitate further practical applications of this sensor, selectivity, repeatability, and long-term stability were considered. The
selectivity of the \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}/\mathrm{SPCE}\) against some biological interferents (including NaCl, Urea, UA, DA and Glucose) was evaluated using an amperometric method. The data in Fig. 6a show that a significant current response was obtained when AA was added, while the response from any of the interferents was negligible. The durability of the \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}/\mathrm{SPCE}\) was also investigated. Fig. 6b shows that the device retained approximately \(89.52\%\) of its original current after 10 days of storage, demonstrating good stability. Fig. 6c provides the current responses to \(50~\upmu\mathrm{M}\) AA from six identical \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}/\mathrm{SPCE}\) electrodes. The RSD for the \(\mathrm{I}_{\mathrm{pa}}\) data was \(2.18\%\), indicating good reproducibility (Fig. 6d).
The above test results were obtained under ideal conditions, whereas actual real-world analyses would involve interference by multiple factors. Therefore, we prepared a portable AA detection system based on \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}/\mathrm{SPCE}\), as shown in Fig. 7a. In this device, the signal was conditioned and processed and then transmitted to a smartphone via a Bluetooth wireless link. Fig. 7b shows the portable AA testing system that was fabricated for the noninvasive and real-time analysis of AA in human sweat. The details of this specific composition are provided in Fig. S2 while the advantages of this portable sensing system are explained in more detail in the SI.
Fig. 8a and b present the electrochemical performance of a portable detection system based on a \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}/\mathrm{SPCE}\) electrode in response to varying AA concentrations in a test chamber. The output current is seen to have varied with increases in the amount of AA to produce a typical stepwise plot. It is also evident that low concentrations of AA (down to \(2\;\upmu\mathrm{M}\)) could be detected by this system. The SI to this paper includes a video demonstrating the real-time changes in the AA detection system based on displaying the amperometric response via a smartphone app. These trials indi
cated that a stable current was obtained within 6 s. Fig. 8c demonstrates the correspondence between the AA level and the current response. A linear relationship was obtained in the range of \(2.0{-}200~\upmu\mathrm{M}\) with the equation I \((\upmu\mathrm{A})=0.057\times\mathrm{C_{AA}}\,(\upmu\mathrm{M})-0.062\), with a sensitivity of \(0.814~\upmu\mathrm{A}~\upmu\mathrm{M}^{-1}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\). Additionally, the measured detection limit is \(1~\upmu\mathrm{M}\) \((\mathrm{S}/\mathrm{N}=3)\).
The response current was found to plateau and remain stable, which would be expected to provide accurate results for a portable sensing system. The stabilization time required to restore the system to \(90\%\) of the background current value is defined as the startup time in Fig. S12[56]. When the unit was turned on, the response current did not immediately reach a steady state but the unit was able to demonstrate a relatively stable output within \(45\ s\). Consequently, the portable detection system made with \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}/\mathrm{SPCE}\) sensing element would be able to complete an AA test within 1 min.
The reliability of this portable sensing system was further evaluated by using human sweat as a sample to measure AA levels. According to previous studies[1], the vitamin C content in human sweat ranges from 10 to \(50\ \upmu\mathrm{mol/L}\). In these trials, sweat samples were collected from a healthy 30-year-old man after \(30\,\mathrm{min}\) of running, after which \(10{-}30~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) aliquots of the sweat were transferred to the sensing element chamber, which contained \(170~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of \(0.1\mathrm{~M~}\) NaOH. Note that the addition of the sweat reduced the NaOH concentration to \(0.09\,\mathrm{M}\), which was expected to modify the properties of the AA sensor. The sensitivity of the \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}/\mathrm{SPCE}\) sensor changes depending on the NaOH concentration. Fig. S13 provides the amperometric I-t curves obtained by adding these sweat samples to NaOH solutions with different concentrations. These results
demonstrate that the largest change in amperometric current was obtained when sweat was added dropwise to \(0.1\,\mathrm{~M~NaOH}\). The dilution error was reduced by assessing the sensitivity of the device using a \(0.09\,\mathrm{~M~NaOH}\) solution. Fig. S14 summarizes the amperometric data acquired from the system using different AA concentrations (range of \(2.0{-}200~\upmu\mathrm{M}\)) in the presence of \(0.09\,\mathrm{~M~}\) NaOH. The corresponding calibration curve indicated that the sensitivity of the system was approximately \(0.063\ \upmu\mathrm{A}/\upmu\mathrm{M}\), which was used as the calibration sensitivity of this portable assay system for vitamin C in subsequent sweat tests. (The vitamin C content in human sweat ranges from 10 to \(50\ \upmu\mathrm{mol/L}\)[1], and the relationship between vitamin C content and current within this concentration range can be derived from the results shown in Fig. S14d.)
Fig. 9(a-h) show the response currents of this portable detection system when exposed to various concentrations of AA in sweat at different points during a single day. While there were slight fluctuations in the output current, the results demonstrate that the signal of the portable sensor increased instantaneously when sweat was added to the sensing element. It is also obvious that the AA concentration in the sweat was higher after exercise than before. To further evaluate the reliability and accuracy of this portable sensing system, the AA concentrations obtained from this new device were compared with those determined using a vitamin C enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) kit. Notably, despite some deviations, the general trend shown by the present portable sensor was in good agreement with the results obtained from the ELISA analyzer (Fig. 9i). Thus, the present system based on \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}/\mathrm{SPCE}\) is a promising approach to the analysis of AA in daily life.
## 3. Conclusions
In summary, we synthesized highly crystalline and conductive \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) MOF and assembled it into a portable electrochemical AA sensing system. \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) with good porosity and abundant catalytic sites was used as the enzyme-free AA detection material. Compared with electrodes based on other MOFs or MOF derivatives[19,25,27,28], the \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) electrode exhibited good performance, including high sensitivity, short response time, and low detection limit. The above results demonstrate the feasibility of using a portable sensing system based on the \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}/\mathrm{SPCE}\) electrode for enzyme-free AA detection. The system shows good stability and can be used to detect AA in human sweat in practical applications, and thus it is expected to meet human health monitoring requirements. In future, this type of portable device may be developed into a wearable detection system for intelligent human health monitoring.
